UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 22: Playbuilding', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Sightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to see. Audience position is the row or cluster of seats. Visibility means each important object can be seen from those seats. Staging is where you place people and objects for those seats, not how fancy the room looks. For the key scene, sit the audience in one row along the front of the space. From there they must see three things: the open empty pocket, Alex''s pointing hand, and the key ring once it is revealed at the front edge of the bench. If the bench blocks the ring, move the ring to the front lip, or move the row, until the ring is in view. Do not seat people behind the bench or behind an actor''s back. Draw a simple bird''s-eye plan: seats, pocket, hand, ring. The plan is the evidence. Rearranging a room is optional, with an adult, and must leave exits clear.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nThe audience will watch the lost-key scene from a single row of seats. Which seating and object plan lets them see what matters?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSeat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key. The pocket opens toward that row, and the ring sits on the front edge of the bench rather than behind it. A row behind the bench, or a pocket opened into an actor''s chest, hides the story. The actors knowing where the key is does not help if the seats cannot see it.\n\nWATCH FOR\nA seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown. Visibility is judged from the audience seats, not from the performer''s eyes. Standing further apart does not fix a ring that is on the far side of the bench. Repair the staging by moving the seats or the ring until the three must-see items are in the sightline.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlace the audience for a dropped timetable and name two things they must see. The timetable falls in a corridor. Decide where the row sits, and name two story details that row must be able to see. Do not use the house key or the paint bench. The written seating plan is the evidence. Moving furniture needs an adult and must keep exits clear.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Drama","learning_intention":"Sightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to see. Audience position is the row or cluster of seats. Visibility means each important object can be seen from those seats. Staging is where you place people and objects for those seats, not how fancy the room looks. For the key scene, sit the audience in one row along the front of the space. From there they must see three things: the open empty pocket, Alex''s pointing hand, and the key ring once it is revealed at the front edge of the bench. If the bench blocks the ring, move the ring to the front lip, or move the row, until the ring is in view. Do not seat people behind the bench or behind an actor''s back. Draw a simple bird''s-eye plan: seats, pocket, hand, ring. The plan is the evidence. Rearranging a room is optional, with an adult, and must leave exits clear.","lesson_number":22,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DR4-MAK-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Place the audience for a dropped timetable and name two things they must see. The timetable falls in a corridor. Decide where the row sits, and name two story details that row must be able to see. Do not use the house ke Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using sightline on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown.. Correction: Seat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown. Visibility is judged from the audience seats, not from the performer''s eyes. Standing further apart does not fix a ring that is on the far side of the bench. Repair the staging by moving the seats or the ring until the three must-see items are in the sightline. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating sightline on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with sightline, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about sightline with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for sightline. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for sightline. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","Seat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key","A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"The audience will watch the lost-key scene from a single row of seats. Which seating and object plan lets them see what matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for sightline; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown","Sightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to see","Seat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key","Careful use of audience position with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named audience position, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown","Name audience position only, without linking it to sightline","Apply sightline to new material and name the audience position used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies sightline with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on sightline. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown","Seat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for sightline","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Sightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","A front row can see the empty pocket, the pointing hand, and the key ring"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Place the audience for a dropped timetable and name two things they must see","Retype the worked example with no new example","A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown","Name audience position only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies sightline to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about sightline with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating sightline on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using sightline on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for sightline. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with sightline, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown.. Correction: Seat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-drama-y9-w15-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 Drama 7-10 (2023) alignment to DR4-MAK-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise sightline.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use sightline with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify audience position and explain why it matters.","I can practise visibility independently on a new prompt.","I can notice staging needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to see.","options":["Sightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to see","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA front row can see the empty pocket, the pointing hand, and the key ring. Seats: one row facing the art-room side of the space. Riley''s pocket opens toward that row, not toward the back wall. The bench sits side-on, with the ring on the front edge so the row sees it when Alex points. A blocked version puts the row behind the bench. The actors can see the ring, and the audience sees only wood. If you cannot move furniture, write the plan and mark the three must-see items anyway.\n\nTRY FIRST\nThe audience will watch the lost-key scene from a single row of seats. Which seating and object plan lets them see what matters?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSeat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key. The pocket opens toward that row, and the ring sits on the front edge of the bench rather than behind it. A row behind the bench, or a pocket opened into an actor''s chest, hides the story. The actors knowing where the key is does not help if the seats cannot see it.","explain":"Sightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to see. Audience position is the row or cluster of seats. Visibility means each important object can be seen from those seats. Staging is where you place people and objects for those seats, not how fancy the room looks. For the key scene, sit the audience in one row along the front of the space. From there they must see three things: the open empty pocket, Alex''s pointing hand, and the key ring once it is revealed at the front edge of the bench. If the bench blocks the ring, move the ring to the front lip, or move the row, until the ring is in view. Do not seat people behind the bench or behind an actor''s back. Draw a simple bird''s-eye plan: seats, pocket, hand, ring. The plan is the evidence. Rearranging a room is optional, with an adult, and must leave exits clear.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown. Visibility is judged from the audience seats, not from the performer''s eyes. Standing further apart does not fix a ring that is on the far side of the bench. Repair the staging by moving the seats or the ring until the three must-see items are in the sightline. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A front-row sightline that sees the key contrasted with seats behind the bench.","caption":"Place the audience where the story objects stay visible.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Front row</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Sees pocket, hand, ring</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Behind bench</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Ring hidden by wood</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: A front row can see the empty pocket, the pointing hand, and the key ring. Seats: one row facing the art-room side of th Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: A front row can see the empty pocket, the pointing hand, and the key ring. Seats: one row facing the art-room side of th Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA front row can see the empty pocket, the pointing hand, and the key ring. Seats: one row facing the art-room side of the space. Riley''s pocket opens toward that row, not toward the back wall. The bench sits side-on, with the ring on the front edge so the row sees it when Alex points. A blocked version puts the row behind the bench. The actors can see the ring, and the audience sees only wood. If you cannot move furniture, write the plan and mark the three must-see items anyway. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"The objects the audience must see beside a staging that only the actors can see.","caption":"Judge visibility from the seats, not from the actors.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Must see</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Empty pocket and the ring</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Actors only</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">They see it, row does not</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown. Visibility is judged from the audience seats, not from the performer''s eyes. Standing further apart does not fix a ring that is on the far side of the bench. Repair the staging by moving the seats or the ring until the three must-see items are in the sightline. Here is a more helpful example: A front row can see the empty pocket, the pointing hand, and the key ring. Seats: one row facing the art-room side of the space. Riley''s pocket opens toward that row, not toward the back wall. The bench sits side-on, with the ring on the front edge so the row sees it when Alex points. A blocked version puts the row behind the bench. The actors can see the ring, and the audience sees only wood. If you cannot move furniture, write the plan and mark the three must-see items anyway.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSeat them in a front row that sees the pocket, the point, and the key. The pocket opens toward that row, and the ring sits on the front edge of the bench rather than behind it. A row behind the bench, or a pocket opened into an actor''s chest, hides the story. The actors knowing where the key is does not help if the seats cannot see it.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. A seat behind the bench hides the key even when the actors think it is shown. Visibility is judged from the audience seats, not from the performer''s eyes. Standing further apart does not fix a ring that is on the far side of the bench. Repair the staging by moving the seats or the ring until the three must-see items are in the sightline. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about sightline?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSightlines decide where the audience sits and what they must be able to see. Audience position is the row or cluster of seats. Visibility means each important object can be seen from those seats. Staging is where you place people and objects for those seats, not how fancy the room looks. For the key scene, sit the audience in one row along the front of the space. From there they must see three things: the open empty pocket, Alex''s pointing hand, and the key ring once it is revealed at the front edge of the bench. If the bench blocks the ring, move the ring to the front lip, or move the row, until the ring is in view. Do not seat people behind the bench or behind an actor''s back. Draw a simple bird''s-eye plan: seats, pocket, hand, ring. The plan is the evidence. Rearranging a room is optional, with an adult, and must leave exits clear. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 22: Playbuilding","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Playbuilding","week":17,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA front row can see the empty pocket, the pointing hand, and the key ring. Seats: one row facing the art-room side of the space. Riley''s pocket opens toward that row, not toward the back wall. The bench sits side-on, with the ring on the front edge so the row sees it when Alex points. A blocked version puts the row behind the bench. The actors can see the ring, and the audience sees only wood. If you cannot move furniture, write the plan and mark the three must-see items anyway.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A front-row sightline that sees the key contrasted with seats behind the bench.","caption":"Place the audience where the story objects stay visible.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Front row</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Sees pocket, hand, ring</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Behind bench</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Ring hidden by wood</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"The objects the audience must see beside a staging that only the actors can see.","caption":"Judge visibility from the seats, not from the actors.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Must see</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Empty pocket and the ring</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Actors only</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">They see it, row does not</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-drama-y9-w15-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
